$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -4}g(x)
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -4}\dfrac{\sqrt{x+20}-4}{x+4}\qquad\gray{\text{This is the rule for }x\neq -4}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -4}\dfrac{\sqrt{x+20}-4}{x+4}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt{x+20}+4}{\sqrt{x+20}+4}\qquad\gray{\text{Rationalize}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -4}\dfrac{x+20-4^2}{(x+4)(\sqrt{x+20}+4)}\qquad\gray{\text{Simplify}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -4}\dfrac{\cancel{x+4}}{\cancel{(x+4)}(\sqrt{x+20}+4)}\qquad\gray{\text{Cancel common factors}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -4}\dfrac{1}{(\sqrt{x+20}+4)}
\\\\
&\small\text{(This is allowed because }x\neq -4)
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{-4+20}+4}\qquad\gray{\text{Direct substitution}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1}{8}
\end{align}$

We obtained that if we set $k=\dfrac{1}{8}$, then $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -4}g(x)=g(-4)$, which makes $g$ continuous at $x=-4$.

Since we already saw that $g$ is continuous for any other $x>-20$, we can determine that it's continuous for all $x>-20$.
